<header><p class="enc-kicker">Religion / idea</p><h1 class="enc-title">Fort Walton culture</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 1100–<a href="/years/1600">1600</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/12th-century">12th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Fort Walton culture was a Mississippian archaeological culture of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/florida">Florida</a> Panhandle and south-western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/georgia">Georgia</a>, flourishing from about 1100 until the arrival of Europeans. Its people farmed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maize">maize</a>, built platform mounds such as those at Lake Jackson and lived in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chiefdom">chiefdoms</a>. The historic Apalachee around <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tallahassee">Tallahassee</a> are its direct descendants.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/fort-walton-culture.jpg" alt="Fort Walton culture" width="320" height="232" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Heironymous Rowe · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Fort_Walton_and_Leon-Jefferson_cultures_map_HRoe_2012.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The culture was named by the archaeologist Gordon Willey after the Fort Walton Mound site near Fort Walton Beach, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/florida">Florida</a>, where he had worked; later research concluded that the mound itself was built and used by people of the contemporary <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pensacola">Pensacola</a> culture. The two are told apart by their pottery: Fort Walton potters tempered their clay with sand, grit or grog, while Pensacola potters used the shell tempering usual in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mississippian-culture">Mississippian cultures</a>. So defined, the culture stretched from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aucilla-river">Aucilla River</a> in the east to a transitional zone around Choctawhatchee Bay in the west, and north into the interior of south <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alabama">Alabama</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/georgia">Georgia</a>.</p><p>It arose when local Weeden Island peoples took up intensive <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maize">maize</a> farming, platform mounds for ceremonial, political and religious use, and new kinds of pottery, changes probably influenced by contact with the major Mississippian centres to the north and west. Early archaeologists thought it marked an intrusion of peoples from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mexico">Mexico</a> or from the Mississippian north-west who replaced the Weeden Island peoples, but by the late 1970s this view had generally been discounted.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Organisation and way of life</h2><p>Fort Walton sites are laid out and placed like other Mississippian sites, except in the Tallahassee Hills, where they lie around lakes and swamps rather than rivers. They range from single-family homesteads and multi-family hamlets to small single-mound centres and large multi-mound centres, a hierarchy that suggests one or more paramount <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chiefdom">chiefdoms</a>. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lake-jackson-mounds">Lake Jackson Mounds</a> in Leon County are the largest known ceremonial centre; eight other ceremonial sites are known in the Apalachee Province. Lake Jackson was abandoned around 1500, when the chiefdom's capital moved to nearby <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anhaica">Anhaica</a>, where the expedition of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hernando-de-soto">Hernando de Soto</a> camped in the winter of 1539.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>In the late Fort Walton period, contact with Lamar phase peoples of central <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/georgia">Georgia</a> changed the decoration and making of pottery, a phase known as the Leon-Jefferson culture. In this period the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chiefdom">chiefdoms</a> collapsed, as native populations declined after contact with European explorers and colonisers such as the de Soto expedition. The Fort Walton and Leon-Jefferson peoples are the direct ancestors of the Apalachee.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Fort Walton culture” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The Apalachee emerged from the <b>Fort Walton culture</b>, the Mississippian tradition of the region between the Aucilla and Ochlockonee rivers around modern Tallahassee, from around 1100.</blockquote><a href="/state/apalachee-chiefdom">Apalachee chiefdom</a></li></ul></section>
